asking it would have shipped broken behavior: any t-string containing a nested f-string in its `{...}` expression would mis-tokenize everything after the nested f-string as f-string tokens instead of t-string tokens). Root cause in `src/pytokens/__init__.py`: `FStringState.is_template` was a single flag that wasn't properly saved/restored across nested string levels.\n\nFix (two related issues in the same code path):\n1. `push_fstring_prefix_quote`/`pop_fstring_quote` now save/restore `is_template` alongside the prefix/quote stack, so it's per-nesting-level instead of global.\n2. In `fstring()`, the outer `is_template` was being overwritten *before* being pushed onto the stack (so the stack captured the wrong value) — reordered to push first, then set the new value.\n3. In the `at_fstring_end` handler, the closing token is now built *before* popping the stack, so the closing token itself still reflects the string that's actually closing, not the one it's returning to.","ttft_ms":4943,"type":"result","duration_ms":98691,"uuid":"bf9d790c-3f75-4aa8-a0a9-9029b89ce526","ttft_stream_ms":1030,"time_to_request_ms":53,"time_to_request_from_spawn_ms":4231881,"warm_spare_claimed":false,"time_origin_ms":1788273493394.2163,"queued_turn_count":0}
